    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d488">

                <pb facs="0734" n="660" sameAs="frus:frus1866p1/pg660"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr. Motley.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 179.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1866-04-30"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >April</hi> 30, 1866.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Your very interesting despatch of the 10th
                    instant, No. 160, which treats of the affairs between Austria and Prussia, has
                    been received. It is very lucid, and your conclusion that war is inevitable
                    would seem to be just. On the other hand, I hear from Paris that the controversy
                    is there understood to have been very recently adjusted. Should this happily
                    prove to be the case, the change of the situation must undoubtedly have come
                    from the resistance of popular interests in Prussia to the war policy of Count
                    Bismarck.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. Lothrop Motley,</hi> Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
